{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794659216322,"sessionID":"ses_e25fd45d8ffe2Iv7ljplDZNbln","part":{"partID":"prt_1da0644e9002CdjEUh9sdUk5US","sessionID":"ses_e25fd45d8ffe2Iv7ljplDZNbln","messageID":"msg_1da063957001A67kds8ULRdilk","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a71fe321eb24e010","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# Iteration 1: headless dispatch contract\n\n## Focus\n\nAngle 1: how Vibe runs one task non-interactively. Selected the narrowest evidence-backed reading: the installed `vibe` 3.1.2 CLI, its captured help contract, and the bundled Python/Rust split that serves the headless run. Live dispatch was deliberately deferred: the install has no `config.toml` and no API key in `~/.vibe/`, so a smoke run would test the auth error path, not the dispatch path. Recorded as a missing dependency below.\n\n## Findings\n\n1. Headless mode is entered with `-p`/`--prompt [PROMPT]`, and the prompt may come from the argument, from stdin, or from `--prompt-file PATH`; the same programmatic mode is mandatory for `--agent-socket` (\"--agent-socket needs programmatic mode: pass -p or --prompt-file\"). The help text says \"--prompt-file ... instead of `-p` TEXT or stdin\", while the binary also carries \"Pass either -p TEXT or --prompt-file\" and \"No prompt provided for programmatic mode\", so stdin-as-prompt is documented but not uniformly enforced. [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt:28-41] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/_bin/vibe-rs (strings: \"Pass either -p TEXT or --prompt-file\", \"No prompt provided for programmatic mode\")]\n\n2. The dispatch surface exposes explicit flags for every axis a governed executor needs: model `--model <ALIAS>` (headless-only, new sessions only), agent profile `--agent <NAME>` (builtin `ask`, `accept-edits`, `smart-approve`, `auto-approve`, or a custom `~/.vibe/agents/NAME.toml`), permissions `--auto-approve`/`--yolo`, `--smart-approve`, `--trust`, `--enabled-tools`, `--disabled-tools`, working directory `--workdir` (also `VIBE_CWD`) and `--add-dir`, and limits `--max-turns`, `--max-price`, `--max-tokens`, `--time-limit`. [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt:17-67]\n\n3. Output is selectable: `--output text` (default), `json`, or `streaming`; `--output-dir DIR` additionally writes `export.json` and a copy of the session journal under `session/` on every exit. [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt:30-31,44-45] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/app_server/run_export.py:1-6]\n\n4. `export.json` is a typed record, not a pile of logs: `vibe_version`, `outcome`, `stop_reason`, `error{message,code}`, `session_id`, `usage{input,output,cached,total}`, `cost_usd`, `steps`, redacted `config`, `warnings`, `started_at`/`ended_at`, `limits`, `subagents[]`, and a computed `exit_code`. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/app_server/run_export.py:117-160,178-238]\n\n5. Exit codes are a fixed five-value taxonomy owned by `RunOutcome.exit_code`: `0` finished; `1` usage_error or config_error; `2` infrastructure_failure (model API after retries, or the runtime); `3` turn_limit, token_limit, price_limit, deadline, terminated, length or refusal; `4` aborted by an `--agent-socket` server. `--time-limit` is documented as \"write the export and exit 3\", which ties the documented timeout behaviour to the same taxonomy. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/app_server/run_export.py:32-84] [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt:38-39]\n\n6. A denied tool has no exit code of its own. Headless runs cannot prompt for approval, and the binary carries \"needs approval, which a headless run cannot give\" followed by \"denied\": the denial is a tool result inside the run, so a run whose only problem was a denied tool can still end `finished` with exit 0. A caller that must detect denial has to read the journal/`export.json` or pin the tool set with `--enabled-tools`/`--disabled-tools` or an explicit approval agent. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/_bin/vibe-rs (strings: \"needs approval, which a headless run cannot give\", \"denied\")] [INFERENCE: based on the exit-code taxonomy in run_export.py:32-84 having no denial member, and on the denial being emitted as a tool result]\n\n7. Architecturally `vibe` is a thin launcher, not the agent: `vibe/cli/launcher.py` `execvpe`s a bundled Rust binary (`vibe/_bin/vibe-rs`, Mach-O arm64) and points it at the installed `vibe-app-server` through `VIBE_APP_SERVER_BIN`. The Rust side owns the CLI and the headless loop (`src/headless_run.rs`, `src/headless/turn.rs`, `src/headless/report.rs` appear in the binary), and the Python side owns the app server, session journal and run export. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/cli/launcher.py:8-29,43-74,90-103] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/_bin/vibe-rs (strings: src/headless/*.rs)]\n\n8. The bundled app server also offers a multiplexed surface: `--multiplex` serves \"isolated Unified Harness connections in one stdio process\", and the binary carries a `run-server` command that serves \"many headless runs over JSON-RPC on stdin and stdout (experimental)\" plus `--runs-per-app-server` to cap live runs. That is a second, long-lived dispatch seam beside one-process-per-run print mode. [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt:82-95] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/_bin/vibe-rs (strings: \"Serve many headless runs over JSON-RPC on stdin and stdout (experimental)\", \"--runs-per-app-server\")]\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- Reading the Python package as the CLI implementation: `launcher.py` only execs the Rust binary, so Python is the app-server/session half. The Rust parser has no local source in the wheel, only the help text and binary strings, so CLI-flag claims beyond those captures are `documented, unconfirmed`.\n- A live `vibe -p OK` smoke dispatch: `~/.vibe/` holds only `trusted_folders.toml`, no `config.toml` and no key, so the run would have exercised the auth-error branch (`config_error`, exit 1) rather than the dispatch contract. Deferred to the second allowed smoke dispatch once credentials exist.\n\n## Dead Ends\n\n- Treating `--output json` as the canonical machine record. It is the response channel; `export.json` is the run record. Building an executor around stdout JSON alone would lose `outcome`, `usage`, `cost_usd`, `steps` and `exit_code`.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- Ambiguous input: the stdin prompt source is documented in `--help` but contradicted by the binary's \"Pass either -p TEXT or --prompt-file\". Selected the narrow reading: use `-p TEXT` or `--prompt-file`; treat stdin as unverified.\n- Contradictory evidence: none beyond the stdin item, recorded above.\n- Missing dependencies: no credentials and no `~/.vibe/config.toml`, so no live smoke dispatch this iteration; source and captured help carried the findings.\n- Partial success: source reading and the captured CLI contract succeeded; the live path is unconfirmed. Status `complete` for the source-backed contract, with the live gap named.\n\n## Sources Consulted\n\n- specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/cli/launcher.py\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/app_server/run_export.py\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/app_server/models.py\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/_bin/vibe-rs (strings)\n\n## Assessment\n\n- New information ratio: 1.0\n- Questions addressed: How does Vibe run one task non-interactively: which prompt source, flags, output formats and exit codes does the headless contract expose?\n- Questions answered: How does Vibe run one task non-interactively: which prompt source, flags, output formats and exit codes does the headless contract expose?\n\n## Reflection\n\n- What worked and why: reading the bundled Python half first turned an opaque CLI into a two-process contract (Rust client + app server), and `run_export.py` is the single authoritative exit-code taxonomy, so the contract claims are grounded in code rather than in help prose.\n- What did not work and why: treating `--help` as the full contract. It omits denial behaviour and the JSON-RPC run server, both of which appear only in the binary's strings.\n- What I would do differently: probe `vibe run-server --help` and `vibe mcp --help` early; the subcommand surface is where the long-lived dispatch seam lives.\n\n## Recommended Next Focus\n\nAngle 2: providers, models and the subscription. Read `vibe/core/model_catalog`, `vibe/core/auth` and `vibe/core/llm`, plus `vibe config schema` (already captured at `evidence/vibe-config-schema.json`), to establish the auth paths, the model-alias roster and which usage counter a turn draws on.\n","path":"specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/devpass-deepseek/iterations/iteration-001.md"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/devpass-deepseek/iterations/iteration-001.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/devpass-deepseek/iterations/iteration-001.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\": \"# Iteration 1: headless dispatch contract\\n\\n## Focus\\n\\nAngle 1: how Vibe runs one task non-interactively. Selected the narrowest evidence-backed reading: the installed `vibe` 3.1.2 CLI, its captured help contract, and the bundled Python/Rust split that serves the headless run. Live dispatch was deliberately deferred: the install has no `config.toml` and no API key in `~/.vibe/`, so a smoke run would test the auth error path, not the dispatch path. Recorded as a missing dependency below.\\n\\n## Findings\\n\\n1. Headless mode is entered with `-p`/`--prompt [PROMPT]`, and the prompt may come from the argument, from stdin, or from `--prompt-file PATH`; the same programmatic mode is mandatory for `--agent-socket` (\\\"--agent-socket needs programmatic mode: pass -p or --prompt-file\\\"). The help text says \\\"--prompt-file ... instead of `-p` TEXT or stdin\\\", while the binary also carries \\\"Pass either -p TEXT or --prompt-file\\\" and \\\"No prompt provided for programmatic mode\\\", so stdin-as-prompt is documented but not uniformly enforced. [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt:28-41] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/_bin/vibe-rs (strings: \\\"Pass either -p TEXT or --prompt-file\\\", \\\"No prompt provided for programmatic mode\\\")]\\n\\n2. The dispatch surface exposes explicit flags for every axis a governed executor needs: model `--model <ALIAS>` (headless-only, new sessions only), agent profile `--agent <NAME>` (builtin `ask`, `accept-edits`, `smart-approve`, `auto-approve`, or a custom `~/.vibe/agents/NAME.toml`), permissions `--auto-approve`/`--yolo`, `--smart-approve`, `--trust`, `--enabled-tools`, `--disabled-tools`, working directory `--workdir` (also `VIBE_CWD`) and `--add-dir`, and limits `--max-turns`, `--max-price`, `--max-tokens`, `--time-limit`. [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt:17-67]\\n\\n3. Output is selectable: `--output text` (default), `json`, or `streaming`; `--output-dir DIR` additionally writes `export.json` and a copy of the session journal under `session/` on every exit. [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt:30-31,44-45] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/app_server/run_export.py:1-6]\\n\\n4. `export.json` is a typed record, not a pile of logs: `vibe_version`, `outcome`, `stop_reason`, `error{message,code}`, `session_id`, `usage{input,output,cached,total}`, `cost_usd`, `steps`, redacted `config`, `warnings`, `started_at`/`ended_at`, `limits`, `subagents[]`, and a computed `exit_code`. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/app_server/run_export.py:117-160,178-238]\\n\\n5. Exit codes are a fixed five-value taxonomy owned by `RunOutcome.exit_code`: `0` finished; `1` usage_error or config_error; `2` infrastructure_failure (model API after retries, or the runtime); `3` turn_limit, token_limit, price_limit, deadline, terminated, length or refusal; `4` aborted by an `--agent-socket` server. `--time-limit` is documented as \\\"write the export and exit 3\\\", which ties the documented timeout behaviour to the same taxonomy. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/app_server/run_export.py:32-84] [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt:38-39]\\n\\n6. A denied tool has no exit code of its own. Headless runs cannot prompt for approval, and the binary carries \\\"needs approval, which a headless run cannot give\\\" followed by \\\"denied\\\": the denial is a tool result inside the run, so a run whose only problem was a denied tool can still end `finished` with exit 0. A caller that must detect denial has to read the journal/`export.json` or pin the tool set with `--enabled-tools`/`--disabled-tools` or an explicit approval agent. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/_bin/vibe-rs (strings: \\\"needs approval, which a headless run cannot give\\\", \\\"denied\\\")] [INFERENCE: based on the exit-code taxonomy in run_export.py:32-84 having no denial member, and on the denial being emitted as a tool result]\\n\\n7. Architecturally `vibe` is a thin launcher, not the agent: `vibe/cli/launcher.py` `execvpe`s a bundled Rust binary (`vibe/_bin/vibe-rs`, Mach-O arm64) and points it at the installed `vibe-app-server` through `VIBE_APP_SERVER_BIN`. The Rust side owns the CLI and the headless loop (`src/headless_run.rs`, `src/headless/turn.rs`, `src/headless/report.rs` appear in the binary), and the Python side owns the app server, session journal and run export. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/cli/launcher.py:8-29,43-74,90-103] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/_bin/vibe-rs (strings: src/headless/*.rs)]\\n\\n8. The bundled app server also offers a multiplexed surface: `--multiplex` serves \\\"isolated Unified Harness connections in one stdio process\\\", and the binary carries a `run-server` command that serves \\\"many headless runs over JSON-RPC on stdin and stdout (experimental)\\\" plus `--runs-per-app-server` to cap live runs. That is a second, long-lived dispatch seam beside one-process-per-run print mode. [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt:82-95] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/_bin/vibe-rs (strings: \\\"Serve many headless runs over JSON-RPC on stdin and stdout (experimental)\\\", \\\"--runs-per-app-server\\\")]\\n\\n## Ruled Out\\n\\n- Reading the Python package as the CLI implementation: `launcher.py` only execs the Rust binary, so Python is the app-server/session half. The Rust parser has no local source in the wheel, only the help text and binary strings, so CLI-flag claims beyond those captures are `documented, unconfirmed`.\\n- A live `vibe -p OK` smoke dispatch: `~/.vibe/` holds only `trusted_folders.toml`, no `config.toml` and no key, so the run would have exercised the auth-error branch (`config_error`, exit 1) rather than the dispatch contract. Deferred to the second allowed smoke dispatch once credentials exist.\\n\\n## Dead Ends\\n\\n- Treating `--output json` as the canonical machine record. It is the response channel; `export.json` is the run record. Building an executor around stdout JSON alone would lose `outcome`, `usage`, `cost_usd`, `steps` and `exit_code`.\\n\\n## Edge Cases\\n\\n- Ambiguous input: the stdin prompt source is documented in `--help` but contradicted by the binary's \\\"Pass either -p TEXT or --prompt-file\\\". Selected the narrow reading: use `-p TEXT` or `--prompt-file`; treat stdin as unverified.\\n- Contradictory evidence: none beyond the stdin item, recorded above.\\n- Missing dependencies: no credentials and no `~/.vibe/config.toml`, so no live smoke dispatch this iteration; source and captured help carried the findings.\\n- Partial success: source reading and the captured CLI contract succeeded; the live path is unconfirmed. Status `complete` for the source-backed contract, with the live gap named.\\n\\n## Sources Consulted\\n\\n- specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt\\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/cli/launcher.py\\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/app_server/run_export.py\\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/app_server/models.py\\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/_bin/vibe-rs (strings)\\n\\n## Assessment\\n\\n- New information ratio: 1.0\\n- Questions addressed: How does Vibe run one task non-interactively: which prompt source, flags, output formats and exit codes does the headless contract expose?\\n- Questions answered: How does Vibe run one task non-interactively: which prompt source, flags, output formats and exit codes does the headless contract expose?\\n\\n## Reflection\\n\\n- What worked and why: reading the bundled Python half first turned an opaque CLI into a two-process contract (Rust client + app server), and `run_export.py` is the single authoritative exit-code taxonomy, so the contract claims are grounded in code rather than in help prose.\\n- What did not work and why: treating `--help` as the full contract. It omits denial behaviour and the JSON-RPC run server, both of which appear only in the binary's strings.\\n- What I would do differently: probe `vibe run-server --help` and `vibe mcp --help` early; the subcommand surface is where the long-lived dispatch seam lives.\\n\\n## Recommended Next Focus\\n\\nAngle 2: providers, models and the subscription. Read `vibe/core/model_catalog`, `vibe/core/auth` and `vibe/core/llm`, plus `vibe config schema` (already captured at `evidence/vibe-config-schema.json`), to establish the auth paths, the model-alias roster and which usage counter a turn draws on.\\n\", \"path\": \"specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/devpass-deepseek/iterations/iteration-001.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794659206377,"end":1794659216322}}}}
