{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794659587780,"sessionID":"ses_e25fd45d8ffe2Iv7ljplDZNbln","part":{"id":"prt_1da0c16c4001a2kS4ma4aTQbxV","sessionID":"ses_e25fd45d8ffe2Iv7ljplDZNbln","messageID":"msg_1da0c08030014Xa4Jl6FW5loN8","type":"step-start","snapshot":"6a1eaf9321400c2844b8384d52c7d1d50e700c60"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794659592248,"sessionID":"ses_e25fd45d8ffe2Iv7ljplDZNbln","part":{"partID":"prt_1da0c16c60010B5Mn1q1EBIVhc","sessionID":"ses_e25fd45d8ffe2Iv7ljplDZNbln","messageID":"msg_1da0c08030014Xa4Jl6FW5loN8","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b09651e709cea0a7","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# Iteration 5: skills, slash commands and subagents\n\n## Focus\n\nAngle 5: what skill format Vibe loads and from where, and how its slash-command skills and custom subagents map onto this repository's skills, commands and agent directories. While tracing the subagent surface this iteration also found and corrected an Angle 4 claim about Code Mode.\n\n## Findings\n\n1. The skill format is YAML frontmatter plus a markdown body, parsed by `parse_skill_markdown` into `SkillMetadata`: `name` (lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, max 64), `description` (max 1024), optional `license`, `compatibility`, `metadata`, `allowed-tools`, `user-invocable` and `disable-model-invocation`. The repository's `SKILL.md` files already use this shape. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/skills/models.py:45-90] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/skills/parser.py:116-153]\n\n2. Skills are both model-invoked and user-invoked. The `skill` tool loads a named skill's full `<skill_content>` block plus a sample of its supporting files, and `user-invocable: false` hides a skill from the slash menu while `disable-model-invocation: true` makes it user-only. So **user-invocable skills are Vibe's slash commands** (`/skill-name`), which supersedes the narrow reading that Vibe has no slash-command surface. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/tools/builtins/skill.py:35-45,60-95] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/skills/models.py:72-90,100-135]\n\n3. Discovery order is computed, not directory-walk order: `skill_paths` first (global scope), then project `.vibe/skills` and `.agents/skills`, then user-level skill dirs, deduped by resolved path; the first claimant of a name wins and the loser is reported as shadowed. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/skills/manager.py:228-275]\n\n4. Registry skills are an opt-in second source: `experimental_enable_registry_skills` pulls shared workspace skills from Mistral with version pins and a reserved `latest` alias, materialised on disk and loaded beside local skills. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/skills/models.py:14-36] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/config/vibe_schema.py:967-984]\n\n5. Subagents are a first-class tool: `task(task, agent=\"explore\")` runs a specialized subagent and returns `{response, turns_used, completed}`; the tool has a permission `allowlist` that defaults to `explore` and a `denylist`, so an executor must allow each custom agent explicitly. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/tools/builtins/task.py:15-60]\n\n6. The subagent roster is the agent-profile roster filtered by `agent_type = \"subagent\"`, bounded by `enable_subagents` (default true), `max_subagents` (32) and `max_concurrent_subagents` (32). A governed fan-out pinning many subagents is therefore capped by config, not by the dispatch flag. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/config/vibe_schema.py:1143-1162] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/agents/models.py:28-80]\n\n7. Repository skills map with near-zero effort: point `skill_paths` at `.skilled/skills`, because every skill there is a `SKILL.md` with YAML frontmatter. Extra repository frontmatter keys (`trigger_phrases`, `importance_tier`) are tolerated as `metadata`, and names such as `sk-code` satisfy the name pattern. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/skills/models.py:45-68] [INFERENCE: based on the `.skilled/skills/*/SKILL.md` frontmatter read in earlier iterations and the parser's permissive metadata handling]\n\n8. Repository commands do **not** map one-to-one: `.opencode/commands/**/*.md` are argument-taking slash commands with `$ARGUMENTS` substitution, while Vibe's user-invocable unit is a skill whose body is instructions and has no documented argument placeholder. Converting a command to a skill preserves the workflow but loses argument interpolation. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/skills/models.py:72-90] [SOURCE: .opencode/commands]\n\n9. Repository agents map with a renderer: body → `instructions`, frontmatter → `display_name`/`description`/`safety`, and deep-loop or review leaves get `agent_type = \"subagent\"` plus an entry in the `task` tool allowlist. The `main` name is reserved for hook matching and may not be used by a profile. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/agents/models.py:33-80]\n\n10. Correction to Angle 4: Vibe **does** have a Code Mode equivalent. `run_typescript` is a runtime builtin program tool; calls a program makes carry `parent_call_id` pointing at the `run_typescript` call, and tool descriptions tell the model to page through data \"from a `run_typescript` program as `tools.vibe.list_sessions`\" and to reach subagents as `tools.subagent`. Iteration 4's \"Code Mode: none\" verdict is superseded by \"equivalent, low-medium porting cost\". [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/app_server/_session_peers.py:40-70] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/mistralai_vibe_local_harness/protocol.py:1074-1093]\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- Treating the absence of `.vibe/commands` as proof that Vibe has no slash commands: user-invocable skills are the slash surface, so the correct verdict is \"commands map to skills, without `$ARGUMENTS`\".\n- Reading the `task` tool as an unrestricted subagent dispatch: its default allowlist contains only `explore`, so a custom subagent is invisible until the allowlist names it.\n\n## Dead Ends\n\n- Searching for a command-argument templating feature in the skill parser: there is no `$ARGUMENTS` or equivalent placeholder in `SkillMetadata` or the skill tool.\n- Searching the builtin tool directory for a Code Mode tool: `run_typescript` lives in the Unified Harness runtime, not in `core/tools/builtins`, which is why the first pass missed it.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- Ambiguous input: \"slash-command skills\" could mean builtin TUI commands or user-defined commands. Resolved as user-invocable skills, since that is the only user-extensible slash surface.\n- Contradictory evidence: two claims about skill precedence. `_compute_search_paths` puts `skill_paths` first and `_discover_skills` keeps the first claimant, while the registry-skill config text says project skills win over global ones. Unresolved; the smallest next check is a two-skill collision fixture across `skill_paths` and `.vibe/skills`.\n- Missing dependencies: no live `/skill-name` invocation was observed, so the slash-menu behaviour is source-documented, not observed.\n- Partial success: the format and discovery contract are fully answered; command-argument loss and precedence are named gaps.\n\n## Sources Consulted\n\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/skills/models.py\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/skills/manager.py\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/skills/parser.py\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/tools/builtins/skill.py\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/tools/builtins/task.py\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/agents/models.py\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/config/vibe_schema.py\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/app_server/_session_peers.py\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/mistralai_vibe_local_harness/protocol.py\n- .opencode/commands\n- .skilled/skills\n\n## Assessment\n\n- New information ratio: 0.95\n- Questions addressed: What skill format does Vibe load, and from where? How do its slash-command skills and custom subagents map onto this repository's skills, commands and agent directories?\n- Questions answered: The question as scoped in the strategy covers both halves; answered, with the `$ARGUMENTS` gap and the precedence contradiction named rather than smoothed over.\n\n## Reflection\n\n- What worked and why: reading the skill model's field descriptions answered the slash-command question that Angle 4 got wrong, because `user-invocable` explicitly names `/skill-name`.\n- What did not work and why: searching `core/tools/builtins` for a TypeScript tool. Code Mode lives in the harness package, so the first verdict was a false negative that only surfaced while reading the `task` tool's neighbours.\n- What I would do differently: when a verdict is \"none\", search the harness package and the binary strings before recording it; the strongest correction of this run came from that gap.\n\n## Recommended Next Focus\n\nAngle 6: hooks and safety. Read `core/hooks` plus the Claude/Kimi hook adapters, the sandbox and protected-path modules, and attempt one live `vibe --help`-safe probe of the hook docs URL to confirm the documented hook contract.\n","path":"specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/devpass-deepseek/iterations/iteration-005.md"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/devpass-deepseek/iterations/iteration-005.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/devpass-deepseek/iterations/iteration-005.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\": \"# Iteration 5: skills, slash commands and subagents\\n\\n## Focus\\n\\nAngle 5: what skill format Vibe loads and from where, and how its slash-command skills and custom subagents map onto this repository's skills, commands and agent directories. While tracing the subagent surface this iteration also found and corrected an Angle 4 claim about Code Mode.\\n\\n## Findings\\n\\n1. The skill format is YAML frontmatter plus a markdown body, parsed by `parse_skill_markdown` into `SkillMetadata`: `name` (lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, max 64), `description` (max 1024), optional `license`, `compatibility`, `metadata`, `allowed-tools`, `user-invocable` and `disable-model-invocation`. The repository's `SKILL.md` files already use this shape. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/skills/models.py:45-90] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/skills/parser.py:116-153]\\n\\n2. Skills are both model-invoked and user-invoked. The `skill` tool loads a named skill's full `<skill_content>` block plus a sample of its supporting files, and `user-invocable: false` hides a skill from the slash menu while `disable-model-invocation: true` makes it user-only. So **user-invocable skills are Vibe's slash commands** (`/skill-name`), which supersedes the narrow reading that Vibe has no slash-command surface. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/tools/builtins/skill.py:35-45,60-95] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/skills/models.py:72-90,100-135]\\n\\n3. Discovery order is computed, not directory-walk order: `skill_paths` first (global scope), then project `.vibe/skills` and `.agents/skills`, then user-level skill dirs, deduped by resolved path; the first claimant of a name wins and the loser is reported as shadowed. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/skills/manager.py:228-275]\\n\\n4. Registry skills are an opt-in second source: `experimental_enable_registry_skills` pulls shared workspace skills from Mistral with version pins and a reserved `latest` alias, materialised on disk and loaded beside local skills. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/skills/models.py:14-36] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/config/vibe_schema.py:967-984]\\n\\n5. Subagents are a first-class tool: `task(task, agent=\\\"explore\\\")` runs a specialized subagent and returns `{response, turns_used, completed}`; the tool has a permission `allowlist` that defaults to `explore` and a `denylist`, so an executor must allow each custom agent explicitly. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/tools/builtins/task.py:15-60]\\n\\n6. The subagent roster is the agent-profile roster filtered by `agent_type = \\\"subagent\\\"`, bounded by `enable_subagents` (default true), `max_subagents` (32) and `max_concurrent_subagents` (32). A governed fan-out pinning many subagents is therefore capped by config, not by the dispatch flag. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/config/vibe_schema.py:1143-1162] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/agents/models.py:28-80]\\n\\n7. Repository skills map with near-zero effort: point `skill_paths` at `.skilled/skills`, because every skill there is a `SKILL.md` with YAML frontmatter. Extra repository frontmatter keys (`trigger_phrases`, `importance_tier`) are tolerated as `metadata`, and names such as `sk-code` satisfy the name pattern. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/skills/models.py:45-68] [INFERENCE: based on the `.skilled/skills/*/SKILL.md` frontmatter read in earlier iterations and the parser's permissive metadata handling]\\n\\n8. Repository commands do **not** map one-to-one: `.opencode/commands/**/*.md` are argument-taking slash commands with `$ARGUMENTS` substitution, while Vibe's user-invocable unit is a skill whose body is instructions and has no documented argument placeholder. Converting a command to a skill preserves the workflow but loses argument interpolation. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/skills/models.py:72-90] [SOURCE: .opencode/commands]\\n\\n9. Repository agents map with a renderer: body → `instructions`, frontmatter → `display_name`/`description`/`safety`, and deep-loop or review leaves get `agent_type = \\\"subagent\\\"` plus an entry in the `task` tool allowlist. The `main` name is reserved for hook matching and may not be used by a profile. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/agents/models.py:33-80]\\n\\n10. Correction to Angle 4: Vibe **does** have a Code Mode equivalent. `run_typescript` is a runtime builtin program tool; calls a program makes carry `parent_call_id` pointing at the `run_typescript` call, and tool descriptions tell the model to page through data \\\"from a `run_typescript` program as `tools.vibe.list_sessions`\\\" and to reach subagents as `tools.subagent`. Iteration 4's \\\"Code Mode: none\\\" verdict is superseded by \\\"equivalent, low-medium porting cost\\\". [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/app_server/_session_peers.py:40-70] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/mistralai_vibe_local_harness/protocol.py:1074-1093]\\n\\n## Ruled Out\\n\\n- Treating the absence of `.vibe/commands` as proof that Vibe has no slash commands: user-invocable skills are the slash surface, so the correct verdict is \\\"commands map to skills, without `$ARGUMENTS`\\\".\\n- Reading the `task` tool as an unrestricted subagent dispatch: its default allowlist contains only `explore`, so a custom subagent is invisible until the allowlist names it.\\n\\n## Dead Ends\\n\\n- Searching for a command-argument templating feature in the skill parser: there is no `$ARGUMENTS` or equivalent placeholder in `SkillMetadata` or the skill tool.\\n- Searching the builtin tool directory for a Code Mode tool: `run_typescript` lives in the Unified Harness runtime, not in `core/tools/builtins`, which is why the first pass missed it.\\n\\n## Edge Cases\\n\\n- Ambiguous input: \\\"slash-command skills\\\" could mean builtin TUI commands or user-defined commands. Resolved as user-invocable skills, since that is the only user-extensible slash surface.\\n- Contradictory evidence: two claims about skill precedence. `_compute_search_paths` puts `skill_paths` first and `_discover_skills` keeps the first claimant, while the registry-skill config text says project skills win over global ones. Unresolved; the smallest next check is a two-skill collision fixture across `skill_paths` and `.vibe/skills`.\\n- Missing dependencies: no live `/skill-name` invocation was observed, so the slash-menu behaviour is source-documented, not observed.\\n- Partial success: the format and discovery contract are fully answered; command-argument loss and precedence are named gaps.\\n\\n## Sources Consulted\\n\\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/skills/models.py\\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/skills/manager.py\\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/skills/parser.py\\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/tools/builtins/skill.py\\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/tools/builtins/task.py\\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/agents/models.py\\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/config/vibe_schema.py\\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/app_server/_session_peers.py\\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/mistralai_vibe_local_harness/protocol.py\\n- .opencode/commands\\n- .skilled/skills\\n\\n## Assessment\\n\\n- New information ratio: 0.95\\n- Questions addressed: What skill format does Vibe load, and from where? How do its slash-command skills and custom subagents map onto this repository's skills, commands and agent directories?\\n- Questions answered: The question as scoped in the strategy covers both halves; answered, with the `$ARGUMENTS` gap and the precedence contradiction named rather than smoothed over.\\n\\n## Reflection\\n\\n- What worked and why: reading the skill model's field descriptions answered the slash-command question that Angle 4 got wrong, because `user-invocable` explicitly names `/skill-name`.\\n- What did not work and why: searching `core/tools/builtins` for a TypeScript tool. Code Mode lives in the harness package, so the first verdict was a false negative that only surfaced while reading the `task` tool's neighbours.\\n- What I would do differently: when a verdict is \\\"none\\\", search the harness package and the binary strings before recording it; the strongest correction of this run came from that gap.\\n\\n## Recommended Next Focus\\n\\nAngle 6: hooks and safety. Read `core/hooks` plus the Claude/Kimi hook adapters, the sandbox and protected-path modules, and attempt one live `vibe --help`-safe probe of the hook docs URL to confirm the documented hook contract.\\n\", \"path\": \"specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/devpass-deepseek/iterations/iteration-005.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794659587782,"end":1794659592248}}}}
