ent/status` | `/goal-dsh`, `dsh_goal`, goal brief, verify note | off | Log and continue |\n100: | `session-start-advisories` | `agent/created` (`startup`) | The six warn-only session-start guards | off | Log each failing guard and continue |\n101: | `session-stop-context` | `agent/disposed` | `session-stop.js` autosave | off | Log and continue |\n102: \n103: Context adapters call `agent.inject` or return `additionalContexts`. Their core errors do not block tool calls. They build injected messages and the goal tool with the harness's own `@deepseek-ai/dsh-llm` and `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tools`, which `dsh-llm.mjs` resolves from the running `dsh` binary, because this checkout has no copy of them.\n104: \n105: `dispatch-audit`, `dispatch-preflight-lint`, `git-preflight-advisory`, `mcp-route-guard` and `session-start-advisories` find the checkout from their own file, not the session cwd. A dsh session runs in a lineage or scratch folder, where a cwd-relative rule tree would be empty and the lint would allow every command. `git-message-gate` is a policy gate, so a contract violation or unreadable contract returns `deny`.\n106: \n107: `goal-manage` and `session-compact-context` wait for the `commands`, `tools` and `agents` services through `ctx.inject`. A bundle-level `inject` would hold every guard until those services exist, and this cordis build has no optional form.\n108: \n109: ---\n110: \n111: ## 5. CONFIGURATION\n112: \n113: The bundle registers the prompt advisor unless its row flag `false` is set. The guards patch still writes `true`, which leaves the advisor on in both compositions. The lineage patch does not change the flag. It mounts its marker row, and a lineage leaf stays without a brief because the adapter returns before it asks the advisor.\n114: \n115: ```yaml\n116: - id: repo-guards\n117:   name: ../plugins/repo-guards/index.mjs\n118:   config:\n119:     adapters:\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 120]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"pro