{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794659760190,"sessionID":"ses_e25fd45d8ffe2Iv7ljplDZNbln","part":{"id":"prt_1da0eb83e0016d8pq9eR6HhWSu","sessionID":"ses_e25fd45d8ffe2Iv7ljplDZNbln","messageID":"msg_1da0eab8c001FeK8UQvu0ikX5H","type":"step-start","snapshot":"41def4e5153a316597bd5d0a545fc800d3319570"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794659766517,"sessionID":"ses_e25fd45d8ffe2Iv7ljplDZNbln","part":{"partID":"prt_1da0eb84c001QRt9qn5qWBApae","sessionID":"ses_e25fd45d8ffe2Iv7ljplDZNbln","messageID":"msg_1da0eab8c001FeK8UQvu0ikX5H","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-80cb55918b61814c","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# Iteration 6: hooks and safety\n\n## Focus\n\nAngle 6: which hook system Vibe exposes, at which events, whether a hook can block a tool call, whether the repository's guard set can be expressed there, and what approval and sandbox modes exist. The guard comparison uses the enforcement adapters in `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/README.md`; that bundle carries more than eight adapters, so this iteration graded the eight enforcement guards (spec gate, dispatch preflight, git message gate, MCP route, overlay, env secret, task dispatch, post-edit quality) plus the two turn-stopping guards as a separate advisory class.\n\n## Findings\n\n1. Hooks are declared, not programmed: `.vibe/hooks.toml` (project) or `~/.vibe/hooks.toml` (global) holds `[[hooks]]` entries with `name`, `type`, `command`, optional `match`, `timeout` (default 60s), `strict` and `description`, and the binary's own changelog records hooks as stable since 2.21 with \"no opt-in flag\". [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/hooks/config.py:1-70] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/hooks/models.py:53-100] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/config/harness_files/_harness_manager.py:140-143]\n\n2. Seven events exist: `post_agent`, `pre_tool`, `post_tool`, `pre_agent`, `notification`, `session_start`, `session_end`. Only `pre_tool`, `post_tool` and `pre_agent` accept `strict`, and the model's own comment defines strict as where \"a failing hook may block instead of letting things through\". A hook can therefore block a tool call — at those three events only. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/hooks/models.py:19-41,80-100]\n\n3. Hook provenance and protocol are first-class: `HookSource` distinguishes host, project, project-plugin, global, global-plugin and client hooks, and `HookProtocol` accepts `VIBE`, `CLAUDE_CODE` and `KIMI_CODE`. Claude Code hook scripts and Kimi hooks are therefore usable as a protocol, which is an odd but real porting shortcut for any guard that already exists in a Claude hook form. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/hooks/models.py:44-60]\n\n4. A resolved hook carries execution context, not just a command: `RuntimeHookDefinition` adds `cwd`, `environment`, `order`, `visibility`, `protocol`, `plugin_name`, `declared_name` and `config_file`, and plugin-sourced hooks must name their plugin. That is enough to run a repository guard from its own checkout rather than the session cwd. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/hooks/models.py:104-128]\n\n5. Approval modes are agent safety levels: `SAFE`, `NEUTRAL`, `DESTRUCTIVE`, `SMART`, `YOLO`, with builtin agents `ask`, `accept-edits`, `smart-approve` and `auto-approve` plus `explore` and `lean`; a dispatch selects one with `--agent`, and per-tool permission is `ALWAYS`, `NEVER` or `ASK`. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/agents.py:6-18] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/agents/models.py:15-30] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/tools/models.py:14-18]\n\n6. The workspace boundary is a policy, not a container: `outside_workdir` is `ask` (default) or `deny`, overridable per tool, with allowlisted paths unaffected; `enable_auto_approve` lets an admin forbid YOLO entirely, and project config plus `AGENTS.md` are gated by folder trust (`--trust` for one invocation). [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/config/vibe_schema.py:813-822,900-932]\n\n7. Protected paths escalate to a human regardless of the classifier: writes that would decide what executes next — `.vibe` config, `.git/hooks`, `.git/config`, shell startup files — are held for approval, and the module says plainly it is a heuristic over argument text, not a boundary (\"only an OS sandbox can make that a guarantee\"). [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/mistralai_vibe_local_harness/vibe/_protected_paths.py:1-40]\n\n8. There is no user-facing sandbox mode in 3.1.2: the config schema exposes no sandbox field and `vibe --help` no sandbox flag, while the harness ships a `Sandbox Environment` adapter that runs workspace tools inside a sandbox through a digest-checked helper. Sandboxing is a runtime deployment choice, not a CLI mode a governed executor can turn on per dispatch. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/mistralai_vibe_local_harness/vibe/_sandbox.py:1-20] [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt:17-67]\n\n9. The eight enforcement guards are expressible, with one payload caveat each: spec gate, dispatch preflight, git message gate, MCP route guard, overlay guard, env secret guard and task dispatch guard all run before a named tool, so `pre_tool` + `strict` is the direct analogue; post-edit quality runs after a named tool, so `post_tool` + `strict`. What does not carry over is the OpenCode payload shape (`tool.execute.before/after` arguments) and each guard's state directory, which must move under `.vibe/` or the packet. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/hooks/models.py:19-41] [SOURCE: .dsh/plugins/repo-guards/README.md:80-93]\n\n10. The turn-stopping guards are the real gap: `completion-evidence` and `ask-surface-guard` run on DSH's `agent/turn-stopping`, and Vibe's nearest event is `post_agent`, which cannot be strict. They can report but cannot block a turn, and the goal/session context adapters (`agent/created`, `session/event` compaction, `agent/disposed`) have no Vibe event at all. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/hooks/models.py:19-41] [SOURCE: .dsh/plugins/repo-guards/README.md:84-100]\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- Treating `--auto-approve` as a sandbox: it suppresses prompts, and the only containment is `outside_workdir` plus protected-path escalation.\n- Porting `completion-evidence` as a blocking hook: `post_agent` has no `strict`, so the port degrades to an advisory.\n\n## Dead Ends\n\n- Searching the config schema for a sandbox mode: there is no field, so any \"sandbox mode\" claim would be documentation-only and is marked UNKNOWN for the CLI.\n- Searching the binary for a hook decision JSON schema: the decision protocol is not exposed as strings, so blocking is cited from the `strict` model contract rather than a wire format.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- Ambiguous input: \"the eight repo guards\" names a subset of a 20-adapter bundle. Fixed the set at the eight enforcement adapters listed in the README table and graded the two turn-stopping guards separately.\n- Contradictory evidence: the binary changelog says hooks are \"three types\" while the Python model declares seven. Resolved as stable core three plus host-fired and engine events; both cited.\n- Missing dependencies: no live hook was executed, and the workspace has no `.vibe/hooks.toml`, so blocking behaviour is source-read only.\n- Partial success: hook events, declaration and safety modes are fully answered; the wire format of a hook decision is UNKNOWN.\n\n## Sources Consulted\n\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/hooks/models.py\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/hooks/config.py\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/config/harness_files/_harness_manager.py\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/config/vibe_schema.py\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/agents.py\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/agents/models.py\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/tools/models.py\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/mistralai_vibe_local_harness/vibe/_protected_paths.py\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/mistralai_vibe_local_harness/vibe/_sandbox.py\n- specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt\n- .dsh/plugins/repo-guards/README.md\n\n## Assessment\n\n- New information ratio: 1.0\n- Questions addressed: What hook system does Vibe expose, at which events, and can a hook block a tool call? Can the eight repo guards be expressed there? What are Vibe's approval and sandbox modes?\n- Questions answered: All three parts; the sandbox answer is that no CLI sandbox mode exists, which is an answer rather than a gap.\n\n## Reflection\n\n- What worked and why: grading the guards by their DSH event rather than by name turned a vague \"can we port hooks\" question into a per-event yes/no, and `strict` is the single field that decides blocking.\n- What did not work and why: looking for a sandbox flag. The absence is real and had to be established from both the schema and the CLI capture before it could be stated.\n- What I would do differently: read `_protected_paths.py` before the agent-safety enums; it is the sharpest statement in the package about what the permission system does and does not guarantee.\n\n## Recommended Next Focus\n\nAngle 7: deep-loop executor support. Read `runtime/lib/deep-loop/executor-config.ts` and `runtime/scripts/fanout-run.cjs` for the executor-kind contract, the environment allowlist and the model roster, then state exactly what a `cli-mistral-vibe` kind needs.\n","path":"specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/devpass-deepseek/iterations/iteration-006.md"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/devpass-deepseek/iterations/iteration-006.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/devpass-deepseek/iterations/iteration-006.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\": \"# Iteration 6: hooks and safety\\n\\n## Focus\\n\\nAngle 6: which hook system Vibe exposes, at which events, whether a hook can block a tool call, whether the repository's guard set can be expressed there, and what approval and sandbox modes exist. The guard comparison uses the enforcement adapters in `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/README.md`; that bundle carries more than eight adapters, so this iteration graded the eight enforcement guards (spec gate, dispatch preflight, git message gate, MCP route, overlay, env secret, task dispatch, post-edit quality) plus the two turn-stopping guards as a separate advisory class.\\n\\n## Findings\\n\\n1. Hooks are declared, not programmed: `.vibe/hooks.toml` (project) or `~/.vibe/hooks.toml` (global) holds `[[hooks]]` entries with `name`, `type`, `command`, optional `match`, `timeout` (default 60s), `strict` and `description`, and the binary's own changelog records hooks as stable since 2.21 with \\\"no opt-in flag\\\". [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/hooks/config.py:1-70] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/hooks/models.py:53-100] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/config/harness_files/_harness_manager.py:140-143]\\n\\n2. Seven events exist: `post_agent`, `pre_tool`, `post_tool`, `pre_agent`, `notification`, `session_start`, `session_end`. Only `pre_tool`, `post_tool` and `pre_agent` accept `strict`, and the model's own comment defines strict as where \\\"a failing hook may block instead of letting things through\\\". A hook can therefore block a tool call — at those three events only. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/hooks/models.py:19-41,80-100]\\n\\n3. Hook provenance and protocol are first-class: `HookSource` distinguishes host, project, project-plugin, global, global-plugin and client hooks, and `HookProtocol` accepts `VIBE`, `CLAUDE_CODE` and `KIMI_CODE`. Claude Code hook scripts and Kimi hooks are therefore usable as a protocol, which is an odd but real porting shortcut for any guard that already exists in a Claude hook form. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/hooks/models.py:44-60]\\n\\n4. A resolved hook carries execution context, not just a command: `RuntimeHookDefinition` adds `cwd`, `environment`, `order`, `visibility`, `protocol`, `plugin_name`, `declared_name` and `config_file`, and plugin-sourced hooks must name their plugin. That is enough to run a repository guard from its own checkout rather than the session cwd. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/hooks/models.py:104-128]\\n\\n5. Approval modes are agent safety levels: `SAFE`, `NEUTRAL`, `DESTRUCTIVE`, `SMART`, `YOLO`, with builtin agents `ask`, `accept-edits`, `smart-approve` and `auto-approve` plus `explore` and `lean`; a dispatch selects one with `--agent`, and per-tool permission is `ALWAYS`, `NEVER` or `ASK`. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/agents.py:6-18] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/agents/models.py:15-30] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/tools/models.py:14-18]\\n\\n6. The workspace boundary is a policy, not a container: `outside_workdir` is `ask` (default) or `deny`, overridable per tool, with allowlisted paths unaffected; `enable_auto_approve` lets an admin forbid YOLO entirely, and project config plus `AGENTS.md` are gated by folder trust (`--trust` for one invocation). [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/config/vibe_schema.py:813-822,900-932]\\n\\n7. Protected paths escalate to a human regardless of the classifier: writes that would decide what executes next — `.vibe` config, `.git/hooks`, `.git/config`, shell startup files — are held for approval, and the module says plainly it is a heuristic over argument text, not a boundary (\\\"only an OS sandbox can make that a guarantee\\\"). [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/mistralai_vibe_local_harness/vibe/_protected_paths.py:1-40]\\n\\n8. There is no user-facing sandbox mode in 3.1.2: the config schema exposes no sandbox field and `vibe --help` no sandbox flag, while the harness ships a `Sandbox Environment` adapter that runs workspace tools inside a sandbox through a digest-checked helper. Sandboxing is a runtime deployment choice, not a CLI mode a governed executor can turn on per dispatch. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/mistralai_vibe_local_harness/vibe/_sandbox.py:1-20] [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt:17-67]\\n\\n9. The eight enforcement guards are expressible, with one payload caveat each: spec gate, dispatch preflight, git message gate, MCP route guard, overlay guard, env secret guard and task dispatch guard all run before a named tool, so `pre_tool` + `strict` is the direct analogue; post-edit quality runs after a named tool, so `post_tool` + `strict`. What does not carry over is the OpenCode payload shape (`tool.execute.before/after` arguments) and each guard's state directory, which must move under `.vibe/` or the packet. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/hooks/models.py:19-41] [SOURCE: .dsh/plugins/repo-guards/README.md:80-93]\\n\\n10. The turn-stopping guards are the real gap: `completion-evidence` and `ask-surface-guard` run on DSH's `agent/turn-stopping`, and Vibe's nearest event is `post_agent`, which cannot be strict. They can report but cannot block a turn, and the goal/session context adapters (`agent/created`, `session/event` compaction, `agent/disposed`) have no Vibe event at all. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/hooks/models.py:19-41] [SOURCE: .dsh/plugins/repo-guards/README.md:84-100]\\n\\n## Ruled Out\\n\\n- Treating `--auto-approve` as a sandbox: it suppresses prompts, and the only containment is `outside_workdir` plus protected-path escalation.\\n- Porting `completion-evidence` as a blocking hook: `post_agent` has no `strict`, so the port degrades to an advisory.\\n\\n## Dead Ends\\n\\n- Searching the config schema for a sandbox mode: there is no field, so any \\\"sandbox mode\\\" claim would be documentation-only and is marked UNKNOWN for the CLI.\\n- Searching the binary for a hook decision JSON schema: the decision protocol is not exposed as strings, so blocking is cited from the `strict` model contract rather than a wire format.\\n\\n## Edge Cases\\n\\n- Ambiguous input: \\\"the eight repo guards\\\" names a subset of a 20-adapter bundle. Fixed the set at the eight enforcement adapters listed in the README table and graded the two turn-stopping guards separately.\\n- Contradictory evidence: the binary changelog says hooks are \\\"three types\\\" while the Python model declares seven. Resolved as stable core three plus host-fired and engine events; both cited.\\n- Missing dependencies: no live hook was executed, and the workspace has no `.vibe/hooks.toml`, so blocking behaviour is source-read only.\\n- Partial success: hook events, declaration and safety modes are fully answered; the wire format of a hook decision is UNKNOWN.\\n\\n## Sources Consulted\\n\\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/hooks/models.py\\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/hooks/config.py\\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/config/harness_files/_harness_manager.py\\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/config/vibe_schema.py\\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/agents.py\\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/agents/models.py\\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/core/tools/models.py\\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/mistralai_vibe_local_harness/vibe/_protected_paths.py\\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/mistralai_vibe_local_harness/vibe/_sandbox.py\\n- specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt\\n- .dsh/plugins/repo-guards/README.md\\n\\n## Assessment\\n\\n- New information ratio: 1.0\\n- Questions addressed: What hook system does Vibe expose, at which events, and can a hook block a tool call? Can the eight repo guards be expressed there? What are Vibe's approval and sandbox modes?\\n- Questions answered: All three parts; the sandbox answer is that no CLI sandbox mode exists, which is an answer rather than a gap.\\n\\n## Reflection\\n\\n- What worked and why: grading the guards by their DSH event rather than by name turned a vague \\\"can we port hooks\\\" question into a per-event yes/no, and `strict` is the single field that decides blocking.\\n- What did not work and why: looking for a sandbox flag. The absence is real and had to be established from both the schema and the CLI capture before it could be stated.\\n- What I would do differently: read `_protected_paths.py` before the agent-safety enums; it is the sharpest statement in the package about what the permission system does and does not guarantee.\\n\\n## Recommended Next Focus\\n\\nAngle 7: deep-loop executor support. Read `runtime/lib/deep-loop/executor-config.ts` and `runtime/scripts/fanout-run.cjs` for the executor-kind contract, the environment allowlist and the model roster, then state exactly what a `cli-mistral-vibe` kind needs.\\n\", \"path\": \"specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/devpass-deepseek/iterations/iteration-006.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794659760204,"end":1794659766517}}}}
