{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794659866654,"sessionID":"ses_e25fd45d8ffe2Iv7ljplDZNbln","part":{"partID":"prt_1da105248001JcssqSG1HVyCEY","sessionID":"ses_e25fd45d8ffe2Iv7ljplDZNbln","messageID":"msg_1da103e0f001V5livfuu9Sk5ow","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a829b040c0b18b9f","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# Iteration 8: ACP and other integration seams\n\n## Focus\n\nAngle 8: Vibe speaks the Agent Client Protocol. This iteration reads the ACP agent surface, compares it with print mode as a fan-out dispatch contract, and names the other seams (`run-server`, `--multiplex`, `--agent-socket`, `--output streaming`) that a governed runtime could use.\n\n## Findings\n\n1. ACP is a stdio JSON-RPC agent with a fixed method surface: `initialize`, `authenticate`, `new_session`, `load_session`, `prompt`, `cancel` and `close_session`. `vibe-acp` accepts only `-h`, `-v` and `--setup`, so every ACP behaviour is configured through the protocol and the config layers, not through flags. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/acp/agent.py:475-600,810-1014] [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt:72-81]\n\n2. ACP carries permission round-trips, not denials: the agent calls `client.request_permission` with `allow_once`, `allow_always`, `reject_once` and `reject_always` options. That is the decisive difference from print mode, where a tool needing approval is denied because a headless run cannot prompt. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/acp/agent.py:902-930] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/acp/utils.py:28-120]\n\n3. ACP sessions are persistent and cancellable: `new_session`, `load_session`, `close_session` and `cancel(session_id)` let one long-lived process hold many sessions, resume them and stop a turn without killing the process. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/acp/agent.py:545-600,997-1020]\n\n4. ACP has no per-run exit taxonomy and no `export.json`. Those are print-mode artifacts: `RunOutcome.exit_code` and `write_run_export` live on the headless path. An ACP supervisor would have to derive outcome, usage and cost from protocol events and session state instead. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/app_server/run_export.py:32-84,217-247] [INFERENCE: based on the ACP method surface exposing no export or exit-code channel]\n\n5. Three further seams exist beside either mode: `--output streaming` emits a machine-readable event stream from print mode, `--agent-socket` lets a server own the run and abort it (exit 4), and `run-server` serves \"many headless runs over JSON-RPC on stdin and stdout (experimental)\" with `--runs-per-app-server`. [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt:30-31,69-95] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/_bin/vibe-rs (strings: \"Serve many headless runs over JSON-RPC on stdin and stdout (experimental)\", \"--runs-per-app-server\")]\n\n6. Print mode fits the existing executor contract with no new supervisor: one process per iteration, a fixed exit taxonomy, `export.json` for usage, and the runner's receipts plus post-hoc write containment. ACP fits a different contract: a persistent per-lineage client, a completion signal built from the `prompt` result plus session events, client-side permission policy, cancellation instead of signal kill, and receipts anchored on session ids rather than process ids. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/lib/deep-loop/executor-audit.ts:179-200] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/fanout-run.cjs:3099-3121] [INFERENCE: based on the ACP method surface and the runner's process-shaped audit]\n\n7. Recommendation: print mode is the better first dispatch surface; ACP is the better second one. Print mode keeps the iteration model, exit-code handling and cost record unchanged, while ACP's permission round-trip and resume are real advantages only once the mode has a supervisor that can answer them, and `run-server` should wait until it leaves experimental. [INFERENCE: based on findings 1-6]\n\n8. ACP would change four fan-out mechanics if adopted: one process per lineage instead of per iteration; a new per-iteration completion record derived from protocol events; a permission answerer per lineage that encodes the repo's guard policy; and cancellation semantics that map onto the existing signal handlers rather than replacing them. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/acp/agent.py:902-1014] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/fanout-run.cjs:591-600]\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- Adopting ACP as the first executor surface: it requires a protocol supervisor, a new completion signal and a permission answerer before a single iteration can run, while print mode runs on the existing contract.\n- Using `run-server` now: the binary itself labels it experimental.\n\n## Dead Ends\n\n- Looking for ACP flags to pin a model or workdir: `vibe-acp` has no flags, so those come from config or from the session `new_session` payload, and the exact payload fields are UNKNOWN from the Python surface alone.\n- Looking for an ACP export path: none exists; export is a headless-only artifact.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- Ambiguous input: \"ACP\" could mean the client-side protocol (`acp` package) or the server-side harness. Graded the agent implementation that a supervisor would drive.\n- Contradictory evidence: none.\n- Missing dependencies: no ACP client was run, so the `new_session` payload and event names are UNKNOWN beyond the Python method signatures.\n- Partial success: the comparison and recommendation are complete; the wire-level ACP payloads are not.\n\n## Sources Consulted\n\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/acp/agent.py\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/acp/utils.py\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/acp/entrypoint.py\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/app_server/run_export.py\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/_bin/vibe-rs (strings)\n- specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt\n- .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/lib/deep-loop/executor-audit.ts\n- .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/fanout-run.cjs\n\n## Assessment\n\n- New information ratio: 1.0\n- Questions addressed: What would a cli-mistral-vibe deep-loop executor kind need, and is ACP a better dispatch surface than print mode?\n- Questions answered: Both halves — the executor requirements came from iteration 7, and this iteration answers the ACP-versus-print comparison.\n\n## Reflection\n\n- What worked and why: comparing the two surfaces by their completion signal (exit code plus export versus protocol events) made the recommendation obvious and grounded, instead of a preference between transports.\n- What did not work and why: trying to find ACP flags. There are none, which itself narrows the design: everything a governed dispatch needs must arrive through config or the protocol.\n- What I would do differently: run a scripted ACP `initialize` handshake in a temp directory to capture the exact `new_session` payload; that is the one wire-level gap left before a build phase could start.\n\n## Recommended Next Focus\n\nSynthesis. All eight angles are answered; `research/research.md` should merge the parity table with the corrections from iteration 5, and `research/phase-plan.md` should rank build phases from the porting costs recorded here.\n","path":"specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/devpass-deepseek/iterations/iteration-008.md"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/devpass-deepseek/iterations/iteration-008.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/devpass-deepseek/iterations/iteration-008.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\": \"# Iteration 8: ACP and other integration seams\\n\\n## Focus\\n\\nAngle 8: Vibe speaks the Agent Client Protocol. This iteration reads the ACP agent surface, compares it with print mode as a fan-out dispatch contract, and names the other seams (`run-server`, `--multiplex`, `--agent-socket`, `--output streaming`) that a governed runtime could use.\\n\\n## Findings\\n\\n1. ACP is a stdio JSON-RPC agent with a fixed method surface: `initialize`, `authenticate`, `new_session`, `load_session`, `prompt`, `cancel` and `close_session`. `vibe-acp` accepts only `-h`, `-v` and `--setup`, so every ACP behaviour is configured through the protocol and the config layers, not through flags. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/acp/agent.py:475-600,810-1014] [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt:72-81]\\n\\n2. ACP carries permission round-trips, not denials: the agent calls `client.request_permission` with `allow_once`, `allow_always`, `reject_once` and `reject_always` options. That is the decisive difference from print mode, where a tool needing approval is denied because a headless run cannot prompt. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/acp/agent.py:902-930] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/acp/utils.py:28-120]\\n\\n3. ACP sessions are persistent and cancellable: `new_session`, `load_session`, `close_session` and `cancel(session_id)` let one long-lived process hold many sessions, resume them and stop a turn without killing the process. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/acp/agent.py:545-600,997-1020]\\n\\n4. ACP has no per-run exit taxonomy and no `export.json`. Those are print-mode artifacts: `RunOutcome.exit_code` and `write_run_export` live on the headless path. An ACP supervisor would have to derive outcome, usage and cost from protocol events and session state instead. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/app_server/run_export.py:32-84,217-247] [INFERENCE: based on the ACP method surface exposing no export or exit-code channel]\\n\\n5. Three further seams exist beside either mode: `--output streaming` emits a machine-readable event stream from print mode, `--agent-socket` lets a server own the run and abort it (exit 4), and `run-server` serves \\\"many headless runs over JSON-RPC on stdin and stdout (experimental)\\\" with `--runs-per-app-server`. [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt:30-31,69-95] [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/_bin/vibe-rs (strings: \\\"Serve many headless runs over JSON-RPC on stdin and stdout (experimental)\\\", \\\"--runs-per-app-server\\\")]\\n\\n6. Print mode fits the existing executor contract with no new supervisor: one process per iteration, a fixed exit taxonomy, `export.json` for usage, and the runner's receipts plus post-hoc write containment. ACP fits a different contract: a persistent per-lineage client, a completion signal built from the `prompt` result plus session events, client-side permission policy, cancellation instead of signal kill, and receipts anchored on session ids rather than process ids. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/lib/deep-loop/executor-audit.ts:179-200] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/fanout-run.cjs:3099-3121] [INFERENCE: based on the ACP method surface and the runner's process-shaped audit]\\n\\n7. Recommendation: print mode is the better first dispatch surface; ACP is the better second one. Print mode keeps the iteration model, exit-code handling and cost record unchanged, while ACP's permission round-trip and resume are real advantages only once the mode has a supervisor that can answer them, and `run-server` should wait until it leaves experimental. [INFERENCE: based on findings 1-6]\\n\\n8. ACP would change four fan-out mechanics if adopted: one process per lineage instead of per iteration; a new per-iteration completion record derived from protocol events; a permission answerer per lineage that encodes the repo's guard policy; and cancellation semantics that map onto the existing signal handlers rather than replacing them. [SOURCE: ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/acp/agent.py:902-1014] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/fanout-run.cjs:591-600]\\n\\n## Ruled Out\\n\\n- Adopting ACP as the first executor surface: it requires a protocol supervisor, a new completion signal and a permission answerer before a single iteration can run, while print mode runs on the existing contract.\\n- Using `run-server` now: the binary itself labels it experimental.\\n\\n## Dead Ends\\n\\n- Looking for ACP flags to pin a model or workdir: `vibe-acp` has no flags, so those come from config or from the session `new_session` payload, and the exact payload fields are UNKNOWN from the Python surface alone.\\n- Looking for an ACP export path: none exists; export is a headless-only artifact.\\n\\n## Edge Cases\\n\\n- Ambiguous input: \\\"ACP\\\" could mean the client-side protocol (`acp` package) or the server-side harness. Graded the agent implementation that a supervisor would drive.\\n- Contradictory evidence: none.\\n- Missing dependencies: no ACP client was run, so the `new_session` payload and event names are UNKNOWN beyond the Python method signatures.\\n- Partial success: the comparison and recommendation are complete; the wire-level ACP payloads are not.\\n\\n## Sources Consulted\\n\\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/acp/agent.py\\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/acp/utils.py\\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/acp/entrypoint.py\\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/app_server/run_export.py\\n- ~/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/_bin/vibe-rs (strings)\\n- specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt\\n- .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/lib/deep-loop/executor-audit.ts\\n- .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/fanout-run.cjs\\n\\n## Assessment\\n\\n- New information ratio: 1.0\\n- Questions addressed: What would a cli-mistral-vibe deep-loop executor kind need, and is ACP a better dispatch surface than print mode?\\n- Questions answered: Both halves — the executor requirements came from iteration 7, and this iteration answers the ACP-versus-print comparison.\\n\\n## Reflection\\n\\n- What worked and why: comparing the two surfaces by their completion signal (exit code plus export versus protocol events) made the recommendation obvious and grounded, instead of a preference between transports.\\n- What did not work and why: trying to find ACP flags. There are none, which itself narrows the design: everything a governed dispatch needs must arrive through config or the protocol.\\n- What I would do differently: run a scripted ACP `initialize` handshake in a temp directory to capture the exact `new_session` payload; that is the one wire-level gap left before a build phase could start.\\n\\n## Recommended Next Focus\\n\\nSynthesis. All eight angles are answered; `research/research.md` should merge the parity table with the corrections from iteration 5, and `research/phase-plan.md` should rank build phases from the porting costs recorded here.\\n\", \"path\": \"specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/devpass-deepseek/iterations/iteration-008.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794659865160,"end":1794659866654}}}}
